/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Take n moles of ideal gas in a piston–cylinder.
   - For a reversible adiabatic step of an ideal gas TVγ−1 is constant (γ = CP/CV), so TH V₂γ−1 = TC V₃γ−1 and TH V₁γ−1 = TC V₄γ−1.
   - QH is heat taken from the hot reservoir; QC is heat rejected to the cold reservoir, both in kJ; TH and TC are absolute temperatures in kelvin.
   - First Carnot theorem: no engine working between two fixed reservoirs can be more efficient than a reversible engine between the same two reservoirs.
   - For any reversible engine, the ratio QH/QC depends only on the two reservoir temperatures.
   - ηC is the Carnot efficiency; W is net work output; QH is heat taken from the hot reservoir; QC is heat rejected; TH and TC are absolute temperatures in kelvin.
   - For the reversed cycle, the same engine becomes a refrigerator or heat pump.
   - COPR is the refrigerator COP; QC is heat removed from the cold space; W is work input; TH and TC are absolute temperatures.
   - COPHP is the heat-pump COP; QH is heat delivered to the hot space.
   - The formula ηC = 1 − TC/TH has three practical readings.
*/
