/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - In the Carnot cycle (see Carnot cycle), heat QH enters at TH and QC leaves at TC.
   - Any reversible cycle can be sliced into thin Carnot strips, and each strip obeys the same result.
   - dS is the change in entropy of the system (J/K); δQrev is the small heat added along a reversible path (J); T is the absolute temperature of the system in kelvin at which that heat crosses.
   - For a finite change, ΔS = ∫₁² δQrev/T, and the value depends only on states 1 and 2, never on the route.
   - Because S is a state function, choose any convenient reversible path between the same two states.
   - CV is the molar heat capacity at constant volume, J/(mol·K); R = 8.314 J/(mol·K) is the gas constant; V₁ and V₂ are initial and final volumes; T is in kelvin.
   - CP is the molar heat capacity at constant pressure, J/(mol·K); P₁ and P₂ are initial and final pressures; T is in kelvin.
   - With constant heat capacity these integrate to ΔS = CV ln(T₂/T₁) + R ln(V₂/V₁) and ΔS = CP ln(T₂/T₁) − R ln(P₂/P₁).
   - ΔH is the latent heat of the phase change in J/mol at temperature T in K (melting, boiling or sublimation).
   - the second form holds for a solid or liquid heated with constant CP and no phase change; T₁ and T₂ are in kelvin.
*/
