/* book exam traps: Using the real irreversible heat in dS = δQ/T. The actual Q/T is not the entropy change; compute ΔS along a reversible substitute between the same end states. | Putting the system temperature instead of the boundary temperature in ∮δQ/T. For an irreversible process the T belongs to the reservoir where the heat crosses. | Swapping the signs of the two ideal-gas forms: the volume term is +R ln(V₂/V₁), the pressure term is −R ln(P₂/P₁). Using plus with pressure gives the wrong sign in compression. | Forgetting that freezing and condensation have negative ΔS, because their ΔH is negative and ΔS = ΔH/T keeps that sign. | Unit slip: R = 8.314 J/(mol·K) gives ΔS in J/K. With R = 0.08314 L·bar/(mol·K) the answer would be in L·bar/K, which is not an entropy unit you can add to J/K. */
